     <h1 class="title" title="Dharamsala Hill Station">Dharamsala</h1>
     <div class="entry-content">
<p>

<img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/createweb/INDIAHILLSTATION.INFO/images/north-india-hill-stations/Dharamsala-hill-station.jpg" title="Dharamsala hill station" alt="Dharamsala hill station, hill stations in india, north india hill stations, Dharamsala" style="width:200px;height:150px" class="thumb alignright"/>


<b>Location:</b> Himachal Pradesh<br />
<b>Area:</b> 29sq.Kms<br />
<b>Altitude:</b> 1380m above sea level.<br />
<b>Climate:</b> Summer 16&#176;C - 26&#176;C; Winter 3&#176;C - 6&#176;C .<br />
<b>Best Time to Visit:</b> March - June,September - October</p>     

<p><b>Main Attractions:</b> Dal Lake,Shrine of Bhagsunath, Kangra Art Museum, Kunal Pathri, Norbulinka Institute, Andretta, Triund  </p>

<p><b>Queen of the hills</b> of Dharamsala, the <b>town of Blue Mountains</b> is a main township of the Kangra region. Dharamshala standing in a foot of snow wrapped up <b>Dhauladhar range</b> and offers spectacular views of snowy peaks, deodar and pine forests, tea gardens and rolling hills. Numerous rivers, fresh healthy air, attractive and close to the snowline, Dharamsala has everything a perfect vacation. It is full of life and yet peaceful.</p>
<h3>Prime Attractions of Dharmsala (Dharamshala):  </h3>
<strong>Kangra Art Museum</strong>
<p>This treasure trove of the Kangra valley's arts, crafts, and rich past, displays artifacts that date back to the 5th century.  </p>
<strong>Dal Lake </strong>
<p>Surrounded by high and green <b>Deodar trees</b> is the lake, which fills a mountain bowl. This lake is easily approachable by road and makes an enchanting and <b>serene picnic spot</b>.  </p>
<strong>St. John's Church </strong>
<p>Under the shade of <b>Doedar branches</b> It was built in 1852 and is dressed in grey stone with some fine Belgian stained glass windows donated by Lady Elgin.  </p>
<strong>Tatwani & Machhrial</strong>
<p>There are hot springs situated at Tatwani, 25-km from Dharamsala but on the way, at Machhrial, is a waterfall twice as big as the one near the <b>Bhagsunath temple</b>.  </p>
<strong>The Shrine of Bhagsunath</strong>
<p>It is the ancient temple of Bhagsunath. There are many fresh water springs close to the temple, which are considered sacred by the Hindus.  </p>
<strong>Kunal Pathri</strong>
<p>These are the rock temples from which the place derives its name.  </p>
<strong>Dharmkot</strong>
<p>Located on the crest of a hill lie this attractive picnic spot, which presents a panoramic view of the Kangra valley and Dauladhar ranges.  </p>
<strong>Norbulinka Institute </strong>
<p>This place has heavy Japanese influence. The shady paths, wooden bridges, small streams tiny water falls make this place look like heaven. The nunnery close to the institute is a place where women are taught the advanced levels of Buddhist philosophy.  </p>
<strong>Chinmaya Tapovan </strong>
<p>The tranquil ashram includes a 9m high image of Hanuman, a Ram temple, a meditation hall, a school and a health & recreation center.  </p>
<strong>Andretta</strong>
<p>It houses a gallery of some of his major works and a pottery center.  </p>
<strong>McLeodganj</strong>
<p>Originally home of the semi-nomadic Gaddi tribe, McLeodganj is today the residence of His Holiness, the Dalai Lama.  </p>
<strong>Masrur</strong>
<p>Fifteen richly carved monolithic rock temples sculpted in the splendid style of the <b>Kailash temple at Ellora</b> and dating back to the 8th century is to be found at Masrur.  </p>
<strong>Triund </strong>
<p>The area is on the foothills of Dhauladhar range. The breathtaking views of the mountains, and the valleys makes Triund an ideal picnic spot and trekking spot.  </p>
<strong>Trilokpur</strong>
<p>The unique cave temples with a stalactite and stalagmites dedicated to Lord Shiva </p>
